WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:17.445
Hello! Welcome back to Superstars in History.

2
00:00:18.345 --> 00:00:23.129
I'm Faye Moss, here to interview another famous face from the past.

3
00:00:24.469 --> 00:00:29.673
Today's guest was the forty-ninth prime minister of the United Kingdom.

4
00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:38.173
But this prime minister was also an important first: she was the first woman to hold this office.

5
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:42.834
Straight from history, here is Margaret Thatcher!

6
00:00:57.799 --> 00:00:58.599
Thank you, Faye.

7
00:00:59.439 --> 00:01:02.560
Shall I start with the landslide victory that brought me to power?

8
00:01:03.796 --> 00:01:06.237
How about we talk about your childhood first?

9
00:01:07.137 --> 00:01:11.858
Your father, Alfred Roberts, was a huge influence for you, right?

10
00:01:12.879 --> 00:01:15.339
Yes, and I owe so much to him!

11
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:22.661
My father owned a grocery store in Grantham, England, the small town where I was born in 1925.

12
00:01:23.662 --> 00:01:28.543
We lived above the shop, and my older sister and I helped out in the store after school.

13
00:01:29.483 --> 00:01:31.984
It was hard work, but we didn't mind at all.

14
00:01:33.032 --> 00:01:35.614
My father inspired my interest in politics.

15
00:01:36.595 --> 00:01:39.978
He was an elected member of the town council and served as mayor.

16
00:01:40.838 --> 00:01:46.903
He wanted to give me all the opportunities he didn't have, and I was determined to make him

17
00:01:46.983 --> 00:01:50.386
proud. I'm sure he was proud!

18
00:01:51.246 --> 00:01:58.232
You received a scholarship to Oxford in 1943, one of the world's most prestigious universities!

19
00:01:59.356 --> 00:02:04.421
I studied chemistry so that I could find a good job when I graduated, but my true passion

20
00:02:04.441 --> 00:02:05.882
was politics, of course.

21
00:02:06.843 --> 00:02:10.586
I quickly became active in the university's Conservative Party club.

22
00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:14.890
One day toward the end of my studies, I was chatting with friends.

23
00:02:15.911 --> 00:02:19.994
One said, "What you really want to do is to be a member of parliament."

24
00:02:20.795 --> 00:02:26.080
Without even thinking I said, "Yes, I do want to be an MP."

25
00:02:27.244 --> 00:02:32.288
When you first ran for a seat in the House of Commons, a body of the UK parliament,

26
00:02:32.528 --> 00:02:37.833
you were only 24 and the youngest female candidate in 1950!

27
00:02:38.854 --> 00:02:41.376
Yes, I ran as a Conservative Party candidate.

28
00:02:42.396 --> 00:02:46.019
I knew I wouldn't win the seat, but it was exciting to campaign.

29
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.385
After my second defeat in 1951, I married Denis Thatcher, a successful businessman.

30
00:02:53.305 --> 00:02:56.208
I began studying law, something I'd always wanted to do.

31
00:02:57.392 --> 00:03:03.635
In 1953 our twins, Mark and Carol, were born, and I filled out the application for my final

32
00:03:03.675 --> 00:03:05.956
law exams while still in the hospital.

33
00:03:07.217 --> 00:03:10.678
Being a mother was important, but I also wanted a career.

34
00:03:11.639 --> 00:03:16.981
I worked as a lawyer, and finally in 1959, I was elected as an MP.

35
00:03:18.422 --> 00:03:22.764
Many people wondered if a young mother could handle a political career.

36
00:03:23.692 --> 00:03:27.955
Did you have any idea that you would become prime minister?

37
00:03:27.975 --> 00:03:32.758
I certainly never believed a woman would be prime minister in my lifetime.

38
00:03:33.899 --> 00:03:40.023
In 1959 only 25 of the 630 MPs were women, and I was the youngest.

39
00:03:40.984 --> 00:03:46.548
Nonetheless I was determined to prove myself, and within two years I became a junior minister.

40
00:03:46.568 --> 00:03:53.957
I was promoted to the cabinet in 1970 as education secretary by Ted Heath, the prime minister at

41
00:03:53.977 --> 00:03:54.497
the time.

42
00:03:55.618 --> 00:04:01.903
Our party was defeated in the 1974 general election, but Heath stayed on as our party's leader.

43
00:04:02.783 --> 00:04:07.146
Many MPs were unhappy with him, but no one seemed willing to challenge him.

44
00:04:08.387 --> 00:04:09.868
I didn't hesitate!

45
00:04:10.749 --> 00:04:16.093
When I was elected as party leader the next year, my victory surprised everyone!

46
00:04:17.194 --> 00:04:20.316
That also made you the leader of the opposition party.

47
00:04:21.144 --> 00:04:23.265
But how did you become prime minister?

48
00:04:24.045 --> 00:04:27.287
In parliament I made a motion of no confidence in the government.

49
00:04:28.187 --> 00:04:31.429
By 1979 Britain was in dire condition.

50
00:04:32.329 --> 00:04:39.272
The economy was terrible with high unemployment, workers on strike, food shortages, power outages, and trash piled in

51
00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:39.992
the streets.

52
00:04:41.053 --> 00:04:47.936
My motion passed by one vote, leading to a general election, which my party won in a landslide!

53
00:04:48.996 --> 00:04:52.338
At age 53 I became prime minister and moved into No.

54
00:04:52.378 --> 00:04:56.961
10 Downing Street with firm ideas on how to solve the country's many crises.

55
00:04:57.921 --> 00:05:01.143
I wanted to cut government spending and give fewer handouts.

56
00:05:02.044 --> 00:05:05.766
People could improve their situations through hard work, just as I had done.

57
00:05:06.746 --> 00:05:08.487
Britain needed to modernize!

58
00:05:09.688 --> 00:05:12.790
Your changes weren't exactly popular.

59
00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:16.672
Unemployment continued, and the economy struggled.

60
00:05:17.588 --> 00:05:20.730
Change was necessary, and recovery would take time.

61
00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.492
I had set my course, even though it was unpopular.

62
00:05:25.653 --> 00:05:32.516
In 1982, when Argentina invaded the Falkland Islands, a British territory, I immediately sent troops there.

63
00:05:33.397 --> 00:05:38.179
Britain and Argentina were at war for 74 days until Argentina withdrew.

64
00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:43.242
We were victorious, and the country was filled with pride and patriotism!

65
00:05:44.123 --> 00:05:46.044
The economy had also started to boom.

66
00:05:47.364 --> 00:05:50.967
In 1983 I was reelected in another landslide.

67
00:05:51.868 --> 00:05:55.330
You were called the most powerful woman in the world.

68
00:05:56.251 --> 00:05:59.693
Another nickname you received was the Iron Lady.

69
00:05:59.713 --> 00:06:06.938
A Soviet newspaper called me that when I first warned about the Soviet Union's attempts at world dominance.

70
00:06:07.819 --> 00:06:14.264
But since iron is strong and unbending, like me, I enjoyed being called the Iron Lady.

71
00:06:15.188 --> 00:06:17.509
Later, during the Cold War, I worked with U.S.

72
00:06:17.569 --> 00:06:21.291
President Reagan to put pressure on the Soviet Union to reform.

73
00:06:22.231 --> 00:06:28.273
When the Soviets shot down a South Korean airliner, killing everyone aboard, President Reagan and I vowed to

74
00:06:28.313 --> 00:06:30.014
be strong against aggressors.

75
00:06:30.934 --> 00:06:34.116
Near the end of my premiership, the Cold War ended.

76
00:06:35.416 --> 00:06:38.838
You faced some significant dangers while you were in office.

77
00:06:40.018 --> 00:06:44.240
In 1984 you survived an assassination attempt!

78
00:06:45.212 --> 00:06:49.235
Terrorists planted a bomb in a hotel where I was staying for a conference.

79
00:06:50.256 --> 00:06:53.718
I was unhurt, but five people were killed and dozens were wounded.

80
00:06:54.619 --> 00:06:58.261
The next day I gave a speech to show that I would not be intimidated.

81
00:06:59.542 --> 00:07:03.204
I led my party to a third election victory in 1987.

82
00:07:04.165 --> 00:07:09.829
Though I had critics and was sometimes unpopular, I was more confident than ever in what I was

83
00:07:09.929 --> 00:07:14.252
doing. Unfortunately some members of my party disagreed.

84
00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:19.081
Eventually another MP challenged your leadership.

85
00:07:19.941 --> 00:07:24.843
Betrayal! I wanted to stay on, but it was clear that the party wanted a new leader.

86
00:07:25.783 --> 00:07:28.384
Politics can be cruel and unpredictable.

87
00:07:29.304 --> 00:07:33.265
In November 1990 I stepped down as prime minister and party leader.

88
00:07:34.185 --> 00:07:39.707
Though I was bitter about resigning, I was proud of all I had accomplished during my wonderful years

89
00:07:39.767 --> 00:07:40.347
in office.

90
00:07:41.367 --> 00:07:43.228
What an amazing career!

91
00:07:44.172 --> 00:07:45.893
Thank you so much for joining us.

92
00:07:46.934 --> 00:07:49.016
We'll let you get back to history now.

93
00:07:50.117 --> 00:07:51.498
My pleasure, Faye.

94
00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:59.764
Margaret Thatcher continued to serve as an MP until 1992.

95
00:08:00.384 --> 00:08:05.428
That year she was appointed to the House of Lords as Baroness Thatcher.

96
00:08:06.309 --> 00:08:12.517
She received the Order of Merit and became a Lady of the Garter, two of the highest honors

97
00:08:12.657 --> 00:08:14.157
given to British citizens.

98
00:08:15.277 --> 00:08:22.779
Baroness Thatcher remained a public figure until her death in 2013 at the age of 87.

99
00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:31.562
Though Margaret Thatcher was sometimes controversial, she was admired for her strong, decisive leadership.

100
00:08:32.542 --> 00:08:38.044
She remains the longest-serving prime minister of the United Kingdom in the modern era.

101
00:08:38.928 --> 00:08:43.011
Since her time, more women have become involved in British politics.

102
00:08:44.031 --> 00:08:45.452
That's all for today's show.

103
00:08:46.293 --> 00:08:50.015
I'll see you next time on Superstars in History.
